 ## Overview
-Fourier-space projection operations compute 2D and 3D forward and backward (adjoint) projection operators for tomographic reconstruction by operating exclusively in the Fourier domain [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself by maintaining data in the Real Fast Fourier Transform (RFFT) format throughout the pipeline, avoiding intermediate format conversions, and achieving performance improvements of 1–2 orders of magnitude over libraries like `torch-fourier-slice` [S1, unofficial].
+Fourier-space projection operations compute 2D and 3D forward and backward projections (adjoints) by operating directly in the frequency domain using the Fourier Slice Theorem. Unlike spatial-domain methods that sample voxel grids, this method transforms reconstruction data to Fourier space, applies rotation and translation via phase modulation, and interpolates non-integer frequency coordinates. It distinguishes itself from spatial back-projection [Back-projection](2450-back-projection.md) and explicit matrix methods [Explicit Radon Matrix Construction](9109-explicit-radon-matrix-construction.md) by offering high performance on CPU, CUDA, and Apple Silicon while supporting end-to-end differentiability for all inputs [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Reconstruction Volume:** A real-space tensor representing the 3D or 2D object [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Projection Data:** A tensor representing the 2D projections in RFFT format [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Geometry:** Rotation matrices and translation vectors defining the pose of each projection.
-*   **Interpolation Type:** Linear (bilinear/trilinear) or Cubic (Catmull-Rom) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Reconstruction Volume:** Real-space tensor, shifted to center at index 0.
+*   **Projections:** Real-space tensor, shifted to center at index 0.
+*   **Geometry:** Rotation matrices and translation vectors.
+*   **Output:** Forward projection yields a sinogram; Backward projection yields a volume.
 
-**Data Structures and Layouts:**
-The library uses PyTorch's RFFT format (following FFTW conventions), where the last spatial dimension is halved plus one to store real-valued symmetry [S1, unofficial]. All spatial dimensions must be even.
